vm-conditions — Condition system in the byte-code VM (plan-2, split from vm-5/vm-8).

Make HANDLER-BIND / HANDLER-CASE (hence IGNORE-ERRORS) and the RESTART system work in
compiled code.  This is the last language gap in the VM and the BLOCKER for wiring
COMPILE globally to emit byte-code (vm-8 part 2).

CONTEXT / WHY IT'S MISSING
- HANDLER-BIND, HANDLER-CASE, RESTART-BIND, RESTART-CASE are kernel SPECIAL OPERATORS
  (sources/eval.c ~1300-1450) but are NOT in SPECIAL-OPERATOR-P's list (prelude ~6598).
  So %VM-ANALYZE treats (handler-bind ...) / (handler-case ...) as ordinary function
  CALLS — the handler frame is never established, and a compiled fn using them COMPILES
  but the handler silently never fires (errors escape).  Confirmed with a compiled
  (ignore-errors (error "x")) — the error escapes.

DESIGN (builds directly on vm-5's dynamic-frame machinery in bytecode.c)
- HANDLER-BIND is the easy one: it is a dynamic frame with NO cleanup and is NEVER a
  longjmp target — the SIGNAL machinery (conditions.c) walks g_frame_stack, finds
  frame_handler_bind frames, and CALLS the matching handler in the signalling context
  (no unwind unless the handler itself exits).  So model it as a VmDyn kind:
  HANDLER_BIND_OPEN pops a bindings list ((type-class . handler-fn) ...) and pushes a
  Frame{type=frame_handler_bind, parameters.handler_bind.bindings=...}; on normal exit
  pop; on unwind-through pop + continue the cascade (exactly the dbind pattern minus the
  restore).  Codegen builds the bindings list at runtime: for each (type fn), push
  (cons (find-class 'type) <handler-fn>); a :dframe :handler scope makes a lexical exit
  pop it.  find-class is exposed; the handler-fns are ordinary (compiled) closures.
- HANDLER-CASE is the harder one: a distinct frame_handler with clause resolution +
  longjmp-on-match (eval.c) — when a matching condition is signalled, control UNWINDS to
  the handler-case and runs the chosen clause with the condition bound.  Two options:
  (a) implement frame_handler in the VM analogous to CATCH (setjmp; the signaller sets
      matched_clause+condition then longjmps; on arrival, bind the condition var and run
      the clause); or (b) since HANDLER-CASE = HANDLER-BIND + BLOCK + RETURN-FROM in the
      classic expansion, add a Lisp-level macro that rewrites HANDLER-CASE to that form
      (which the VM already compiles once HANDLER-BIND works) and register it so the
      analyzer expands it before seeing the special op.  Option (b) is far less VM work
      — prefer it; keep the eval.c special op for the tree-walker.
- RESTART-BIND / RESTART-CASE / INVOKE-RESTART: same shape (a registry frame +, for
  restart-case, a longjmp-on-invoke).  Do after handler-*.  ("must-restart" acceptance.)

ACCEPTANCE: compiled HANDLER-CASE catches a signalled ERROR and runs the right clause
with the condition bound; IGNORE-ERRORS returns (values nil condition); HANDLER-BIND
runs a handler that declines (falls through) and one that transfers control; a compiled
RESTART-CASE + INVOKE-RESTART round-trips; parity with the tree-walker on the condition
tests; full suite green.  Then vm-8 part 2 can wire COMPILE globally.

DEPENDS ON: vm-5 (NLX frames), vm-6 (closures — handlers are closures).

====================================================================
PROGRESS (v1.8.11, branch vm) — HANDLER-BIND + HANDLER-CASE + IGNORE-ERRORS done.

- HANDLER-BIND is a VM special form now (analyzer recognizes it via
  %VM-SPECIAL-OPERATOR-P, which adds handler-bind/handler-case to SPECIAL-OPERATOR-P's
  list).  Codegen builds the ((type-class . handler-fn) ...) list at runtime — the type
  is a class-name symbol resolved with FIND-CLASS — and BC_HANDLER_BIND_OPEN pushes a
  frame_handler_bind (a no-cleanup dynamic VmDyn: never a longjmp target, so on an
  unwind-through it just pops + continues the cascade; BYTECODE_VERSION -> 7).  SIGNAL
  (conditions.c, shared with the tree-walker) walks g_frame_stack and CALLS a matching
  handler in the signalling context — so handlers that decline (return) fall through and
  handlers that transfer control (THROW / RETURN-FROM) just work.  A :dframe :handler
  control scope makes a lexical exit pop the frame.
- HANDLER-CASE is EXPANDED by the analyzer (analyzer-local — the tree-walker's own
  eval.c special op is untouched) into BLOCK/RETURN-FROM + CATCH/THROW + HANDLER-BIND
  with capturing handler closures: a matching handler stashes the condition and THROWs a
  clause index to a fresh-cons tag; the CATCH returns it and a CASE runs the clause with
  the condition bound.  Handles :NO-ERROR (via MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL).  IGNORE-ERRORS (a
  prelude macro -> handler-case) therefore works and returns (values nil condition).
- FIXED the block-multiple-values limitation as part of this: BLOCK / RETURN-FROM now
  generate their value in mv-context (%VM-GEN-FOR-VALUES) and :block is an mv-buffer
  setter, so BLOCK preserves ALL values (needed since IGNORE-ERRORS returns 2 values
  through the handler-case block).
- Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_conditions — ignore-errors (caught + ok),
  handler-case catch/bind/normal/type-dispatch/nested, handler-bind transfer + decline,
  and block-MV.  Full suite green.

REMAINING for this issue: the RESTART system (RESTART-BIND / RESTART-CASE /
INVOKE-RESTART — same frame shape, frame_restart) for the "must-restart" acceptance.
NOT a blocker for vm-8 part 2 if COMPILE uses a SAFE FALLBACK (compile with the VM;
fall back to minimal compilation when the analyzer errors) — the analyzer should ERROR
on the still-unsupported kernel special operators (restart-*) so they trigger fallback
rather than miscompile.

====================================================================
DONE — RESTART system (v1.8.21, branch vm).  vm-conditions is now COMPLETE.

RESTART-BIND is a VM special form: BC_RESTART_BIND_OPEN pops a runtime restarts list
((name fn interactive-fn test-fn) ...) and pushes a frame_restart — structurally a
HANDLER-BIND (a no-cleanup dynamic VmDyn, VMK_RESTART_BIND; never a longjmp target, so an
unwind-through just pops + continues the cascade; BYTECODE_VERSION -> 10).  Codegen
(%vm-gen-restart-bind) builds the specs at runtime; the analyzer (%vm-analyze-restart-bind)
parses :interactive-function / :test-function; a :dframe :restart control scope makes a
lexical exit pop the frame.  FIND-RESTART / INVOKE-RESTART / COMPUTE-RESTARTS (conditions.c)
are SHARED with the tree-walker and already walk g_frame_stack for frame_restart, so they
find the VM's frame with no new code.

RESTART-CASE is analyzer-EXPANDED (%vm-expand-restart-case, exactly parallel to
HANDLER-CASE) into RESTART-BIND + BLOCK/RETURN-FROM + CATCH/THROW: each clause becomes a
restart-bind restart whose function stashes the invocation args and THROWs the clause index
to a fresh-cons tag; the CATCH returns the index and a CASE runs that clause's body with its
(full ordinary) lambda-list bound to the args via APPLY.  So RESTART-CASE needs no new VM
opcode.

BUG FIXED (shared, was latent): INVOKE-RESTART decided "apply a RESTART-BIND fn in place vs
unwind to a RESTART-CASE frame" by (functionp || closurep || built_in_function_p) — which
EXCLUDES byte-code CompiledFunction / CompiledClosure.  So a COMPILED restart-bind function
(and every RESTART-CASE clause's throwing CLOSURE) was mistaken for a RESTART-CASE restart
and longjmp'd into the frame_restart, which is NOT a target (it is a HANDLER-BIND-style
pass-through) -> the cascade ran off the end ("unwind_next_frame: no next frame").  Fixed by
also accepting compiled_function_p / compiled_closure_p (conditions.c).

ACCEPTANCE MET: compiled RESTART-CASE + INVOKE-RESTART round-trips (normal + invoked), binds
the clause lambda-list (incl. &rest) to the invocation args, is invocable from a HANDLER-BIND
handler, selects the right clause among several, nests, and MATCHES the tree-walker;
FIND-RESTART / COMPUTE-RESTARTS see the compiled restart.  test_vm_restarts.  Full 42-suite
green, -std=c17 -Wpedantic -Wextra clean.

KNOWN LIMITATION (orthogonal, pre-existing vm-6 gap, NOT restart-specific): an escaping
RETURN-FROM out of an inner closure to an outer BLOCK does not byte-compile (COMPILE falls
back to interpreted).  A RESTART-BIND function that transfers via such a RETURN-FROM thus
runs interpreted; one that transfers via THROW / a restart-case clause compiles.
====================================================================
